# LF in the working tree, everywhere. The repo default is autocrlf=true, which
# means a CRLF working copy is produced BY CHECKOUT -- so every file a tool
# writes and nobody ever checks out (generated bindings, anything an agent
# authored) stays LF, and the tree ends up mixed. Nothing reaches history that
# way (the index is LF regardless), but it makes any byte-level comparison
# between a freshly generated file and a checked-out one fail on line endings
# alone, which the three rules below were each added to patch after it happened.
# Pinning the whole tree removes the class instead of the instances: tool output
# and checkout output are now the same bytes.
#
# Existing CRLF working copies convert on their next checkout, not on adding this
# line. `git status` stays clean throughout, because the clean filter normalises
# either form to the LF already in the index.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts are the exception, pre-emptively: cmd.exe mis-parses a LF-only
# batch file (a split label or `goto` target silently changes control flow), and
# PowerShell's signature blocks are CRLF-sensitive. None of these are tracked
# today -- the rule is here so that adding the first one cannot quietly inherit
# the wrong ending.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf

# --- kept deliberately, though the rule above now covers them ----------------
#
# Each of these paths has a gate that compares BYTES, so for them the ending is
# not cosmetic but load-bearing, and saying so where the reader is looking beats
# making them infer it from a blanket rule. If the global pin above is ever
# loosened, these must survive it.

# The published wire schema is generated by `python -m nvda_mcp_wire.schema`,
# which emits LF-terminated JSON, and the CI drift gate compares it against the
# committed copy on windows-latest.
specs/wire/v1/schema.json text eol=lf

# Same reasoning one layer up: wiregen writes LF, so its committed golden output
# is pinned too, or the golden test compares LF against a CRLF checkout.
server/tools/wiregen/testdata/* text eol=lf

# And the generated binding itself: `scripts/drift.py` compares it with
# read_bytes() either side of a regeneration, so a CRLF checkout would report
# drift when nothing about the contract changed.
server/adapters/wire/*.gen.go text eol=lf
